## Summary

The xpack-platform plugin extends Seqera Platform with features that ship outside the open-source Nextflow distribution. This version provides the **global cache**: a cross-pipeline, cross-run task reuse system backed by cloud object storage. The global cache turns a cloud storage prefix into a shared, content-addressable cache where the work directory itself is the cache, shared across runs, pipelines and users. Unlike `-resume`, which reuses work from a previous run of the same pipeline, tasks are reused whenever any other run has already executed the same work, regardless of who ran it or which pipeline it belonged to. The plugin is supported on Amazon S3, Azure Blob Storage, and Google Cloud Storage, and requires a license (`NXF_XPACK_LICENSE`).

## Get Started

Add the plugin and point it at a cloud prefix:

```groovy
plugins {
    id &#x27;xpack-platform&#x27;
}

globalCache {
    enabled      = true
    path         = &#x27;s3://my-bucket/cache&#x27;
    fileIdentity = &#x27;provenance&#x27;
}
```

Then run as usual — there is no `-resume` flag to pass, because reuse is not scoped to a session:

```bash
nextflow run main.nf
```

**Important:** `globalCache.path` derives the work directory, so do not set `-w` or `workDir`.

### Key Configuration Options

- **`globalCache.enabled`** — Enable the global cache (default: `false`). Requires Nextflow 26.10.6 or later.
- **`globalCache.path`** — The shared cloud storage prefix used as the cache (e.g., `s3://bucket/cache`). Must be a cloud URI.
- **`globalCache.fileIdentity`** — How a task input is identified across runs (default: `provenance`):
  - `provenance`: inputs are identified by the task that produced them using metadata lookups
  - `sample`: inputs are identified by a fixed-cost sample of content (head, middle, tail windows plus byte size)
- **`globalCache.secondaryPaths`** — An ordered list of read-only secondary cache prefixes consulted when a task is not found in the primary cache (default: `[]`)

### Rules and Gotchas

- Do not set `-w` or `workDir` — the work directory is derived from `globalCache.path`
- `cleanup = true` is rejected — the work directory is the cache
- Task caching must stay on — `cache = false` and `process.cache = false` both abort the run
- `-resume` is accepted but means less than usual — matching is by task content, not by session
- Every bucket must be reachable from wherever the tasks run

## Examples

### Amazon S3

```groovy
plugins {
    id &#x27;xpack-platform&#x27;
}

globalCache {
    enabled      = true
    path         = &#x27;s3://my-bucket/cache&#x27;
    fileIdentity = &#x27;sample&#x27;

    sample {
        threshold = &#x27;1 MB&#x27;
        size      = &#x27;128 KB&#x27;
    }
}

aws {
    region = &#x27;eu-west-1&#x27;
}
```

### Google Cloud Storage

```groovy
plugins {
    id &#x27;xpack-platform&#x27;
}

globalCache {
    enabled = true
    path    = &#x27;gs://my-bucket/cache&#x27;
}

google {
    project  = &#x27;my-project&#x27;
    location = &#x27;us-central1&#x27;
}
```

### Azure Blob Storage

```groovy
plugins {
    id &#x27;xpack-platform&#x27;
}

globalCache {
    enabled = true
    path    = &#x27;az://my-container/cache&#x27;
}

azure {
    storage {
        accountName = &#x27;mystorageaccount&#x27;
        accountKey  = System.getenv(&#x27;AZURE_STORAGE_KEY&#x27;)
    }
}
```

### Sharing a Read-Only Cache

```groovy
globalCache {
    enabled        = true
    path           = &#x27;s3://my-bucket/cache&#x27;
    fileIdentity   = &#x27;sample&#x27;
    secondaryPaths = [&#x27;s3://org-shared/cache&#x27;]
}
```

